fast-forward push. Increase push timeout only after shrinking history if needed. [Task 2]\n- The episodes state machine is `IDEA_LOCKED → STORYBOARD_LOCKED → VISUAL_CALIBRATED → PRODUCTION_PASSED → PUBLISH_READY → PUBLISHED → DATA_REVIEWED`; transitions are adjacent-only and auto-validate before state changes. Use `episode_state.py init/show/transition`, `validate_episode.py <dir> [--target X] [--all]`, and `test_validator.py`. `episode-state.json` is authoritative over README prose. [Task 3]\n- Import a delivery package by inspecting its original nested path and a prior import commit, flattening it into the matching existing series, renaming it to match the story, updating root/episodes/series/system indexes, and spot-checking `SHA256SUMS` with `Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256`. Stage tracked changes via `git add -u` plus the new directory, then `git push origin story`; do not stage ignored package images. [Task 3]\n- The media whitelist permits `episodes/**/assets/characters/**`, `research/competitors/**`, and `research/account/**`; binary/media extensions are otherwise ignored. [Task 3]\n\n## Failures and how to do differently\n\n- `git rebase --autostash origin/story` can fail because ignored, untracked images would be overwritten. Abort it, preserve the worktree, and use a controlled merge/conflict resolution or the verified tree-rewrite route; never delete the ignored media merely to make rebase succeed. [Task 2]\n- Removing files from the index does not remove their old blobs: a push can still contain ~GB of past `images`/ZIP data. Measure outgoing objects and squash/reparent only after tree-equivalence verification. [Task 2]\n- A prior subtitle comparison used the wrong S1/S2 sources. Final-script identification is a required first check; docs drafts and old helpers are not proof of published text. [Task 1]\n- Delete/rename cleanup can leave root README links stale: after removing a template or changing a package path, `rg` the repository for old references a